Kiln's vendor rknpu driver: take the compute clock from the device tree

Kiln pins CLK_RKNN_DSU0 to 594 MHz on every power-up, with a comment saying why:
devfreq is off, nothing else sets the compute clock, and at the CRU power-on
default a large matmul cannot finish inside the job timeout.

That is the right bring-up default and the wrong experimental one. r390 needed
the vendor arm and the charsiu arm at the SAME clock, and with the rate
hardcoded they would have differed whenever the charsiu DTB pinned anything
else -- two variables in one cell, which is the mistake v12's 13/14 made and
this project spent a day undoing.

So the rate comes from assigned-clock-rates when the node says one, and falls
back to 594 MHz when it does not. Every existing device tree keeps the old
behaviour.

THIS FILE IS NOT UNDER VERSION CONTROL ANYWHERE ELSE. ~/Documents/kiln is not
a git repository and the vendor source is fetched rather than vendored, so this
patch is the only record that the change exists. Re-running
driver/fetch-vendor-driver.sh will overwrite it.

--- a/driver/rknpu/rknpu_drv.c
+++ b/driver/rknpu/rknpu_drv.c
@@ -1065,7 +1065,20 @@
 	 * Pin a clean GPLL-derived rate (594 MHz = 1188/2) on every power-up and log
 	 * the resulting rates for bring-up.
 	 */
-	clk_set_rate(rknpu_dev->clks[0].clk, 594000000);
+	{
+		/*
+		 * KILN: the rate comes from the device tree when it says one, so
+		 * an experiment can hold this side at the same clock as the open
+		 * driver's arm. A hardcoded 594 made the vendor arm and the
+		 * charsiu arm two different frequencies whenever the charsiu DTB
+		 * pinned anything else, which is two variables in one cell.
+		 */
+		u32 dt_rate = 0;
+
+		if (of_property_read_u32_index(dev->of_node,
+					       "assigned-clock-rates", 0,
+					       &dt_rate) || !dt_rate)
+			dt_rate = 594000000;
+		clk_set_rate(rknpu_dev->clks[0].clk, dt_rate);
+	}
 	LOG_DEV_INFO(dev, "kiln clk: dsu0=%lu aclk0=%lu aclk1=%lu\n",
 		     clk_get_rate(rknpu_dev->clks[0].clk),
 		     clk_get_rate(rknpu_dev->clks[1].clk),
